 T1 \u2192 no\n- T2 \u2192 yes\n- T8 \u2192 yes\n- T9 \u2192 yes\n- B1 \u2192 yes\n- B6 \u2192 no (B6 not reversible)\n- B7 \u2192 no\n- B9 \u2192 yes\n- J3 \u2192 yes\n\nSo reversible: T2, T8, T9, B1, B9, J3 \u2192 6\nNon-reversible: W7, T1, B6\u00d72, B7 \u2192 5\n\nIf I discard W7, T1, B7 \u2014 all non-reversible, then hand becomes: T2, T8, T9, B1, B6\u00d72, B9, J3\n\nThen reversible: T2, T8, T9, B1, B9, J3 \u2192 6\nStill B6\u00d72 not reversible.\n\nB6 is not in list \u2014 so cannot achieve Reversible Tiles.\n\nMust discard something.\n\nBest candidate: **W7** \u2014 it's the only Character tile, doesn't fit any major pattern, not reversible, not terminal/honor for outside hand, not helping chows.\n\nWhereas T1,T2 could form chow, B6\u00d72 is foundation for pung/kong, T8,T9 could form chow, B9 could be in 789, etc.\n\nAlso, Seat Wind is North (F3), Prevailing South (F2), neither in hand.\n\nJ3 is White Dragon \u2014 could be useful.\n\nBut W7 is completely dead.\n\nMoreover, discarding W7 avoids blocking potential patterns.\n\nSo discard W7.\n\nAnswer: Play W7\n\nFinal decision.\n\nSituation: I have a mixed hand with a chow in Bamboos, a pair of B6, and various terminals. The only tile not contributing to any potential scoring pattern (such as All Chows, Reversible Tiles, or Half Flush) is W7, which is isolated and from a different suit.\n\nEvaluation: Keeping W7 hinders pursuit of suit-based patterns like Full Flush or Half Flush. Discarding W7 streamlines the hand toward Bamboo/Dot/honor combinations. Other tiles like T1, T2, T8, T9 support possible chows; B6\u00d72 can become a pung or kong; B1, B9, J3 are terminals/honors that may contribute to patterns like Outside Hand or dragon melds.\n\nReason: W7 is the least useful tile, offering no synergy with existing tiles or viable winning patterns. Removing it improves hand flexibility and efficiency.\n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
