 used in an exposed [T7,T8,T9] chow. Retaining it only helps if paired, but no second T7 exists (T7 unshown: 1, possibly still in wall or held). Risky to keep lone T7.\n3. **Play T2**: Similar to T3; breaking potential low bamboo chow. But again, limited prospects.\n4. **Play F1 / F3**: Both are singleton winds. F1 has 2 unshown, F3 has 2 unshown. No pung possible unless concealed kong, which isn\u2019t feasible. Better to discard one to move toward All Chows (which excludes honor pairs).\n5. **Play B6**: I have two B6s. One is used in [B5,B6,B7], the other is free. Discarding one B6 is safe since I don\u2019t need four, and I\u2019m not going for Seven Pairs. Keeping one allows flexibility.\n6. **Play B1**: B1 has 2 unshown. It's a terminal, part of outside hand. But holding it blocks clean shape development. If aiming for All Chows, must discard all terminals/honors.\n\nBest path: Aim for **All Chows** (worth 2 points), which requires four chows and a pair of suited tiles. Currently at two chows. Possible third chow: use T2-T3 + T4 (but T4 very scarce), or use B1 with B2-B3? But B2/B3 also mostly gone. Alternatively, maybe form a pair from B6 or T2/T3?\n\nWait pattern: If I keep T2 and T3, I might later draw T4 to form [T2,T3,T4], giving three chows. Then fourth chow could come from B1-B2-B3? But B2/B3 unlikely. Alternatively, already have two chows \u2014 perhaps aim for a pair like B6-B6 and hope to draw into another chow via T2-T3-T4 or similar.\n\nBut options are slim. More realistically, accept that **All Chows is marginal**, but better than Chicken Hand. Need at least 8 points. Current patterns possibly available:\n- **All Chows (2 pts)** \u2014 possible if I can form 4 chows\n- **No Honors (1 pt)** \u2014 must dump F1/F3\n- **One Voided Suit?** I have dots and bamboos, characters barely present \u2192 likely only two suits \u2192 **One Voided Suit (1 pt)**\n- Possibly **Mixed Double Chow (1 pt)** if I get identical number chows\n\nBut best bet: minimize honors, try to complete third chow.\n\nSince **T7** appears only once in hand and its sequence is already completed as a chow, keeping it serves no purpose unless pairing \u2014 but T7 is not easy to pair (only 1 left unseen). Similarly, **T3** and **T2** together offer future potential.\n\nHowever, among valid actions, safest progress is to shed an honor tile.\n\nBetween F1 and F3: F1 has 2 unshown, F3 has 2 unshown \u2014 both relatively safe. But previous player discarded F3 twice, and no one punged \u2014 suggests they might not want it. Still, no evidence of danger.\n\nBetter to keep both winds until forced, because if I go for **Outside Hand (8 pts)**, then having terminal/honor in every group counts. Let\u2019s check:\n\n- Existing melds: [T7,T8,T9] \u2192 contains T7,T8,T9 (T7 and T9 are terminals) \u2192 qualifies as \"outside\" meld\n- [B5,B6,B7] \u2192 middle tiles, B5/B7 are not terminals \u2192 **does NOT qualify** for Outside Hand (since B5 and B6 are simples)\n\nSo [B5,B6,B7] invalidates Outside Hand requirement (every meld must contain terminal or honor). So **Outside Hand impossible**.\n\nThus, honors are liabilities.\n\nTherefore, goal shifts to **All Chows** (requires no honor tiles in hand, and 4 chows). To achieve:\n- Must discard **both F1 and F3**\n- Need to form two more chows\n\nCurrent live tiles: T2, T3, B1, B6\u00d72\n\nCan I make:\n- [T2,T3,T4]? Need T4 (only 1 left)\n- [B1,B2,B3]? B2: 1 left, B3: 1 left \u2014 very tight\n- Or use B6-B6 as pair? Then need third chow from elsewhere\n\nBut I already have two chows. If I can form [T2,T3,T4] and [B1,B2,B3], plus pair B6-B6 \u2192 hand: [[T7,T8,T9], [B5,B6,B7], [T2,T3,T4], [B1,B2,B3]] + B6-B6 \u2014 wait, too many B6s? No: [B5,B6,B7] uses one B6, hand has two B6s \u2192 total three B6s max, okay.\n\nBut problem: B2 and B3 each only 1 left \u2014 likely already taken. Same with T4.\n\nHighly improbable.\n\nAlternative: abandon third chow, instead consider **Half Flush**? But I have both Bamboo and Dots, plus honors \u2014 too mixed.\n\nAlternatively, **Chicken Hand (8 pts)** may be inevitable \u2014 i.e., minimal hand with no valuable patterns beyond base 8.\n\nBut we must play optimally.\n\nGiven the constraints, best action is to reduce useless tiles.\n\nT7 is part of an already-used chow and cannot contribute to any new meld. It is a leftover suited tile not easily paired. Moreover, T7 only has one copy left unseen \u2014 possibly in wall, possibly held. Risk of being a dangerous discard is low.\n\nBut compared to others, **T7** is less useful than T2/T3 (which can form chow), and less necessary than B6 (possible pair), and more expendable than honors which might be kept for discarding later when choice narrows.\n\nWait \u2014 valid actions include **Play T7**, so it's legal.\n\nBut let's compare:\n\n- **Play F1 or F3**: removes an honor, moves toward No Honors (1 pt) and improves chance for All Chows\n- **Play T7**: removes a mid-game tile that doesn\u2019t help chow formation\n- **Play T2 or T3**: breaks potential [T2,T3,T4] chow\n- **Play B1**: terminal, hard to use in chow (needs B2,B3)\n- **Play B6**: I have two, but losing one hurts pair potential\n\nBest discard: **T7**\n\nWhy?\n- T7 cannot form a pung/kong (only 1 left)\n- Cannot form chow unless I draw T5/T6 \u2014 but T6 is gone, T5 has 1 left \u2014 impossible\n- Already used in meld \u2014 keeping extra T7 is redundant\n- Safer than discarding T2/T3 which have chow potential\n- Better than discarding B6 which could be a pair\n- Better than discarding B1 which might be used in edge chow\n- Unlike honors, I can\u2019t score Concealed Hand or Fully Concealed anyway (I\u2019ve Chi\u2019d)\n\nAlso, by discarding **T7**, I signal less interest in T2/T3 sequences, protecting future plays.\n\nFinal decision: **Play T7**\n\nReason: T7 is a stranded tile with no viable path to contribute to a winning pattern. It does not support forming new chows or pairs effectively. Discarding it streamlines the hand toward potential All Chows or Chicken Hand while avoiding blocking useful combinations like [T2,T3,T4].\n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
